body:
Add a comment to explain why this is done:
```suggestion
				// Call `_update_panning()` immediately after starting the stream.
				// This re-sets bus levels to ensure they are correct for the first few frames.
				// (These levels may be initialized incorrectly due to the call to start_playback_stream
				// ignoring the Area3D's bus options.)
				_update_panning();
```

TLDR: instead of calling `_update_panning()` second time in a row, you could just call `AudioServer::get_singleton()->start_playback_stream` with `bus_volumes` instead of `bus_map`.

That being said, `_get_actual_bus()` has slightly different logic than what's at the end of `_update_panning()`, where in `_get_actual_bus()`, an `Area3D` overriding main output bus is ignored if it also has reverb bus send enabled. I'm not sure what's the reason for such discrepancy.

--- 
When the stream starts the panning is updated in the previous block:
```cpp
	if (setplay.get() > 0 || (internal->active.is_set() && last_mix_count != AudioServer::get_singleton()->get_mix_count()) || force_update_panning) {
		force_update_panning = false;
		volume_vector = _update_panning();
	}
```
- at this point we have cached updated pitch, filters and bus volumes;
- when then in the next block `AudioServer::get_singleton()->start_playback_stream` starts the stream, it sets correct pitch, attenuation and filter, **but not the bus volume map**, because the `volume_vector`, returned from the call to `_update_panning()` above, contains only the main bus volume and it is passed to the `start_playback_stream`

It appears that the only missing piece form the pan update is that `bus_volumes`, which contains all the bus sends just computed in the `_update_panning()`.  - instead of `volume_vector` for supposedly main/"actual" bus.
